ogically distinct. This study aimed to compare the gene expression profiles of HPV-negative oropharyngeal squamous cell carcinoma (OPC) and normal benign uvula/tonsil tissues and determine what biological processes and pathways are affected in HPV-negative OPCs.","gpl":"17077","gse":"55547","taxon":"Homo sapiens","entrytype":"GSE","gdstype":"Expression profiling by array","ptechtype":"","valtype":"","ssinfo":"","subsetinfo":"","pdat":"2014/03/05","suppfile":"TXT","samples":[{"accession":"GSM1339060","title":"Analysis 6: HPV Negative - Biological replicate 16"},{"accession":"GSM1339063","title":"Analysis 6: Normal - Biological replicate 3"},{"accession":"GSM1339046","title":"Analysis 6: HPV Negative - Biological replicate 2"},{"accession":"GSM1339052","title":"Analysis 6: HPV Negative - Biological replicate 8"},{"accession":"GSM1339049","title":"Analysis 6: HPV Negative - Biological replicate 5"},{"accession":"GSM1339051","title":"Analysis 6: HPV Negative - Biological replicate 7"},{"accession":"GSM1339045","title":"Analysis 6: HPV Negative - Biological replicate 1"},{"accession":"GSM1339048","title":"Analysis 6: HPV Negative - Biological replicate 4"},{"accession":"GSM1339054","title":"Analysis 6: HPV Negative - Biological replicate 10"},{"accession":"GSM1339056","title":"Analysis 6: HPV Negative - Biological replicate 12"},{"accession":"GSM1339057","title":"Analysis 6: HPV Negative - Biological replicate 13"},{"accession":"GSM1339062","title":"Analysis 6: Normal - Biological replicate 2"},{"accession":"GSM1339053","title":"Analysis 6: HPV Negative - Biological replicate 9"},{"accession":"GSM1339059","title":"Analysis 6: HPV Negative - Biological replicate 15"},{"accession":"GSM1339061","title":"Analysis 6: Normal - Biological replicate 1"},{"accession":"GSM1339050","title":"Analysis 6: HPV Negative - Biological replicate 6"},{"accession":"GSM1339055","title":"Analysis 6: HPV Negative - Biological replicate 11"},{"accession":"GSM1339058","title":"Analysis 6: HPV Negative - Biological replicate 14"},{"accession":"GSM1339064","title":"Analysis 6: Normal - Biological replicate 4"},{"accession":"GSM1339047","title":"Analysis 6: HPV Negative - Biological replicate 3"}],"relations":[],"extrelations":[],"n_samples":20,"seriestitle":"","platformtitle":"","platformtaxa":"","samplestaxa":"","pubmedids":["25899179"],"projects":[],"ftplink":"ftp://ftp.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/geo/series/GSE55nnn/GSE55547/","geo2r":"yes","bioproject":"PRJNA240003"},"200055546":{"uid":"200055546","accession":"GSE55546","gds":"","title":"Gene expression profiling of HPV-active oropharyngeal cancers and Normal benign uvula, tonsil","summary":"Head and neck cancer (HNC) is the fifth most common malignancy worldwide with an annual mortality rate of 200,000. About 90% of HNC can be classified as head and neck squamous cell carcinomas (HNSCC), of which approximately 75% are attributed to alcohol and tobacco consumption and 25 are associated with human papillomavirus (HPV), predominantly HPV16. HPV-associated OPC have better prognosis and a more favorable response to therapy as compared to HPV-negative tumors. Viral oncoproteins are capable of transforming primary human keratinocytes from either genital or oral epithelia in vitro and most likely play the same role in vivo, by disrupting cell-cycle regulatory pathways leading to a genetic progression to ano-genital cancer and OPC. However, the precise mechanisms by which HPV mediates malignant transformation of keratinocytes in the upper digestive tract epithelia are not entirely clear.  HPV E7-mediated inactivation of pRb results in overexpression of p16INK4A, which is commonly used as a clinical surrogate marker for HPV positivity/activity. However, high p16INK4A alone has insufficient sensitivity and specificity as a biomarker of HPV positivity in different mucosal sub-sites of HNC. Therefore, increasing emphasis is being placed on the assessment of viral load and E7 oncogene expression, resulting in further classification of HPV positive OPC as HPV-active and HPV-inactive. Differences in risk factors, age of presentation, clinical behavior and gene expression profiles indicate that HPV-positive and HPV-negative tumors develop via different molecular mechanisms and are biologically distinct. This study aimed to compare the gene expression profiles of HPV-active OPCs and normal benign uvula/tonsil tissues and determine what biological processes and pathways are affected in HPV-active tumors.","gpl":"17077","gse":"55546","taxon":"Homo sapiens","entrytype":"GSE","gdstype":"Expression profiling by array","ptechtype":"","valtype":"","ssinfo":"","subsetinfo":"","pdat":"2014/03/05","suppfile":"TXT","samples":[{"accession":"GSM1339036","title":"Analysis 5: HPV Active - Biological replicate 8"},{"accession":"GSM1339039","title":"Analysis 5: HPV Active - Biological replicate 11"},{"accession":"GSM1339042","title":"Analysis 5: Normal - Biological replicate 2"},{"accession":"GSM1339041","title":"Analysis 5: Normal - Biological replicate 1"},{"accession":"GSM1339044","title":"Analysis 5: Normal - Biological replicate 4"},{"accession":"GSM1339030","title":"Analysis 5: HPV Active - Biological replicate 2"},{"accession":"GSM1339029","title":"Analysis 5: HPV Active - Biological replicate 1"},{"accession":"GSM1339033","title":"Analysis 5: HPV Active - Biological replicate 5"},{"accession":"GSM1339038","title":"Analysis 5: HPV Active - Biological replicate 10"},{"accession":"GSM1339032","title":"Analysis 5: HPV Active - Biological replicate 4"},{"accession":"GSM1339035","title":"Analysis 5: HPV Active - Biological replicate 7"},{"accession":"GSM1339031","title":"Analysis 5: HPV Active - Biological replicate 3"},{"accession":"GSM1339034","title":"Analysis 5: HPV Active - Biological replicate 6"},{"accession":"GSM1339037","title":"Analysis 5: HPV Active - Biological replicate 9"},{"accession":"GSM1339040","title":"Analysis 5: HPV Active - Biological replicate 12"},{"accession":"GSM1339043","title":"An