positive and HPV-negative tumors develop via different molecular mechanisms and are biologically distinct. This study aimed to compare the gene expression profiles of HPV-negative and HPV-inactive OPCs from both African American and European American patients, and determine their biological and racial differences.","gpl":"17077","gse":"55545","taxon":"Homo sapiens","entrytype":"GSE","gdstype":"Expression profiling by array","ptechtype":"","valtype":"","ssinfo":"","subsetinfo":"","pdat":"2014/03/05","suppfile":"TXT","samples":[{"accession":"GSM1339009","title":"Analysis 4: AA - HPV Negative - Biological replicate 1"},{"accession":"GSM1339012","title":"Analysis 4: AA - HPV Negative - Biological replicate 4"},{"accession":"GSM1339015","title":"Analysis 4: AA - HPV Negative - Biological replicate 7"},{"accession":"GSM1339018","title":"Analysis 4: EA - HPV Inactive - Biological replicate 2"},{"accession":"GSM1339021","title":"Analysis 4: EA - HPV Negative - Biological replicate 1"},{"accession":"GSM1339014","title":"Analysis 4: AA - HPV Negative - Biological replicate 6"},{"accession":"GSM1339017","title":"Analysis 4: EA - HPV Inactive - Biological replicate 1"},{"accession":"GSM1339023","title":"Analysis 4: EA - HPV Negative - Biological replicate 3"},{"accession":"GSM1339006","title":"Analysis 4: AA - HPV Inactive - Biological replicate 2"},{"accession":"GSM1339026","title":"Analysis 4: EA - HPV Negative - Biological replicate 6"},{"accession":"GSM1339020","title":"Analysis 4: EA - HPV Inactive - Biological replicate 4"},{"accession":"GSM1339019","title":"Analysis 4: EA - HPV Inactive - Biological replicate 3"},{"accession":"GSM1339005","title":"Analysis 4: AA - HPV Inactive - Biological replicate 1"},{"accession":"GSM1339022","title":"Analysis 4: EA - HPV Negative - Biological replicate 2"},{"accession":"GSM1339008","title":"Analysis 4: AA - HPV Inactive - Biological replicate 4"},{"accession":"GSM1339025","title":"Analysis 4: EA - HPV Negative - Biological replicate 5"},{"accession":"GSM1339011","title":"Analysis 4: AA - HPV Negative - Biological replicate 3"},{"accession":"GSM1339007","title":"Analysis 4: AA - HPV Inactive - Biological replicate 3"},{"accession":"GSM1339024","title":"Analysis 4: EA - HPV Negative - Biological replicate 4"},{"accession":"GSM1339027","title":"Analysis 4: EA - HPV Negative - Biological replicate 7"},{"accession":"GSM1339013","title":"Analysis 4: AA - HPV Negative - Biological replicate 5"},{"accession":"GSM1339028","title":"Analysis 4: EA - HPV Negative - Biological replicate 8"},{"accession":"GSM1339016","title":"Analysis 4: AA - HPV Negative - Biological replicate 8"},{"accession":"GSM1339010","title":"Analysis 4: AA - HPV Negative - Biological replicate 2"}],"relations":[],"extrelations":[],"n_samples":24,"seriestitle":"","platformtitle":"","platformtaxa":"","samplestaxa":"","pubmedids":["25899179"],"projects":[],"ftplink":"ftp://ftp.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/geo/series/GSE55nnn/GSE55545/","geo2r":"yes","bioproject":"PRJNA240001"},"200055543":{"uid":"200055543","accession":"GSE55543","gds":"","title":"Gene expression profiling of African American vs European American HPV-negative oropharyngeal squamous cell carcinomas","summary":"Head and neck cancer (HNC) is the fifth most common malignancy worldwide with an annual mortality rate of 200,000. About 90% of HNC can be classified as head and neck squamous cell carcinomas (HNSCC), of which approximately 75% are attributed to alcohol and tobacco consumption and 25% are associated with human papillomavirus (HPV), predominantly HPV16. HPV-associated OPC have better prognosis and a more favorable response to therapy as compared to HPV-negative tumors. Differences in risk factors, age of presentation, clinical behavior and gene expression profiles indicate that HPV-positive and HPV-negative tumors develop via different molecular mechanisms and are biologically distinct.   African American (AA) males have a higher incidence of HNC than any other racial/gender group, and a mortality rate almost three-fold that observed in European American (EA) males. Overall, AA patients tend to present with more HPV-negative OPC and have worse prognosis as compared to both HPV-positive and HPV-negative HNSCC in EA patients. Despite the unveiling of differential gene expression patterns, genetic and epigenetic profiles and the compilation of a mutational landscape along with preliminary TCGA data of HPV-related and unrelated HNC, the molecular determinants of the racial disparity in HNC are yet to be identified.   This study aimed to compare the gene expression profiles of HPV-negative HNSCC from AA and EA patients, and determine their biological differences.","gpl":"17077","gse":"55543","taxon":"Homo sapiens","entrytype":"GSE","gdstype":"Expression profiling by array","ptechtype":"","valtype":"","ssinfo":"","subsetinfo":"","pdat":"2014/03/05","suppfile":"TXT","samples":[{"accession":"GSM1338978","title":"Analysis 2: European American - Biological replicate 5"},{"accession":"GSM1338981","title":"Analysis 2: European American - Biological replicate 8"},{"accession":"GSM1338967","title":"Analysis 2: African American - Biological replicate 2"},{"accession":"GSM1338966","title":"Analysis 2: African American - Biological replicate 1"},{"accession":"GSM1338969","title":"Analysis 2: African American - Biological replicate 4"},{"accession":"GSM1338975","title":"Analysis 2: European American - Biological replicate 2"},{"accession":"GSM1338972","title":"Analysis 2: African American - Biological replicate 7"},{"accession":"GSM1338968","title":"Analysis 2: African American - Biological replicate 3"},{"accession":"GSM1338971","title":"Analysis 2: African American - Biological replicate 6"},{"accession":"GSM1338976","title":"Analysis 2: European American - Biological replicate 3"},{"accession":"GSM1338973","title":"Analysis 2: African American - Biological replicate 8"},{"accession":"GSM1338977","title":"Analysis 2: European American - Biological replicate 4"},{"accession":"GSM1338970","title":"Analysis 2: African American - Biological replicate 5"},{"accession":"GSM1338974","title":"Analysis 2: European American - Biological replicate 1"},{"accession":"GSM1338980","title":"Analysis 2: European American - Biological replicate 7"},{"accession":"GSM1338979","title":"Analysis 2: European American - Biological replicate 6"}],"relations":[],"extrelations":[],"n_samples":16,"seriestitle":"","platformtitle":"","platformtaxa":"","samplestaxa":"","pubmedids":["25899179"],"projects":[],"ftplink":"ftp://ftp.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/geo/series/GSE55nnn/GSE55543/","geo2r":"yes","bioproject":"PRJNA240002"},"200055487":{"uid":"200055487","accession":"GSE55487","gds":"","title":"Isolation and genomic characterization of cancer stem cells in head and neck cancer","summary":"This study investigated the use of three different established cell sorting strategies to isolate and characterize stem cells from head and neck cancer cell lines. Five low passage cell lines were subjected to cell sorting based on Hoechst side population, Aldefluor and CD44 expression. Isolated cell populations were studied for gene expression, radiosensitivity and chemosensitivity to cis-platin and paclitaxel Each sorting method identified a different set of genes associated with different Gene Ontology categories with mitosis being the only common category. CD44 associated gene changes were almost exclusively associated with cell cycle and in particular mitosis. There were no significant differences in radiosensitivity or cis-platin sensitivity of stem or non-stem cells but CD44 isolated stem cells were more resistant to paclitaxel","gpl":"5175","gse":"55487","taxon":"Homo sapiens","entrytype":"GSE","gdstype":"Expression profiling by array","ptechtype":"","valtype":"","ssinfo":"","subsetinfo":"","pdat":"2014/03/02","suppfile":"CEL","samples":[{"accession":"GSM1337820","title":"UT24A_CD44 High"},{"accession":"GSM1337800","title":"UT24A_ALD positive"},{"accession":"GS