rmtitle":"","platformtaxa":"","samplestaxa":"","pubmedids":["17510386"],"projects":[],"ftplink":"ftp://ftp.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/geo/series/GSE6nnn/GSE6791/","geo2r":"yes","bioproject":"PRJNA99227"},"200007073":{"uid":"200007073","accession":"GSE7073","gds":"","title":"Gene expression profiles of head and neck squamous cell carcinoma (HNSCC) cell lines","summary":"Chromosomal comparative genomic hybridization (CGH) and oligonucleotide microarrays were used to examine 8 HNSCC cell lines and a plot of gene expression levels relative to their position on the chromosome was produced. Three highly up-regulated genes, NT5C3, ANLN and INHBA, were identified on chromosome 7p14. These genes were subjected to quantitative real-time RT-PCR on cDNA and genomic DNA derived from 8 HNSCC cell lines. ANLN and INHBA showed a strong positive correlation between mRNA expression and genomic DNA levels and a similar relationship was shown for the known oncogene, EGFR, at 7p11.2. In clinical samples, ANLN and INHBA showed a significantly higher expression in tumors than in normal tissues. Patients with high expression levels of INHBA had a shorter disease-free survival rate. Therefore, INHBA may be a promising prognostic marker of HNSCC. Keywords: Identification of molecular targets based on genome-wide gene expression profiling","gpl":"1291","gse":"7073","taxon":"Homo sapiens","entrytype":"GSE","gdstype":"Expression profiling by array","ptechtype":"","valtype":"","ssinfo":"","subsetinfo":"","pdat":"2007/03/01","suppfile":"TXT","samples":[{"accession":"GSM169555","title":"HO1N1 cell line"},{"accession":"GSM169558","title":"Sa3 cell line"},{"accession":"GSM169553","title":"Ca9-22 cell line"},{"accession":"GSM169556","title":"HSC2 cell line"},{"accession":"GSM169559","title":"D562 cell line"},{"accession":"GSM169554","title":"H1 cell line"},{"accession":"GSM169557","title":"HSC3 cell line"},{"accession":"GSM169560","title":"FaDu cell line"}],"relations":[],"extrelations":[],"n_samples":8,"seriestitle":"","platformtitle":"","platformtaxa":"","samplestaxa":"","pubmedids":["17982635"],"projects":[],"ftplink":"ftp://ftp.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/geo/series/GSE7nnn/GSE7073/","geo2r":"yes","bioproject":"PRJNA98481"},"200006631":{"uid":"200006631","accession":"GSE6631","gds":"2520","title":"Expression data from head and neck squamous cell carcinoma","summary":"Forty-four paired (from the same patient) samples of head and neck squamous cell carcinoma (HNSCC) and normal tissue were studied with Affymetrix U95A chips. A stringent multi-test approach, combining 7 traditional and microarray-specific statistical tests, was used to analyze the resultant data. Candidate genes were assigned to tiers of significance based on the number of statistical tests that each gene satisfied. Representative genes (both up-regulated and down-regulated) from each of the 3 tiers would be quantified with RT-PCR on both microarray-tested and new samples of HNSCC. The goal of this study is to identify reliable differentially-expressed genes on HNSCC and to testify our hypothesis whether or not a combinatorial approach (multi-tests) to analyzing microarray data can really identify differentially-expressed genes with fewer false-positives. Keywords: disease state; tumor vs. normal","gpl":"8300","gse":"6631","taxon":"Homo sapiens","entrytype":"GSE","gdstype":"Expression profiling by array","ptechtype":"","valtype":"","ssinfo":"","subsetinfo":"","pdat":"2007/01/20","suppfile":"CEL","samples":[{"accession":"GSM153828","title":"Cancer 8"},{"accession":"GSM153848","title":"Cancer 18"},{"accession":"GSM153831","title":"Normal mucosa 10"},{"accession":"GSM153851","title":"Normal mucosa 20"},{"accession":"GSM153854","title":"Cancer 21"},{"accession":"GSM153837","title":"Normal mucosa 13"},{"accession":"GSM153820","title":"Cancer 4"},{"accession":"GSM153840","title":"Cancer 14"},{"accession":"GSM153814","title":"Cancer 1"},{"accession":"GSM153834","title":"Cancer 11"},{"accession":"GSM153817","title":"Normal mucosa 3"},{"accession":"GSM153846","title":"Cancer 17"},{"accession":"GSM153849","title":"Normal mucosa 19"},{"accession":"GSM153823","title":"Normal mucosa 6"},{"accession":"GSM153843","title":"Normal mucosa 16"},{"accession":"GSM153855","title":"Normal mucosa 22"},{"accession":"GSM153826","title":"Cancer 7"},{"accession":"GSM153815","title":"Normal mucosa 2"},{"accession":"GSM153852","title":"Cancer 20"},{"accession":"GSM153829","title":"Normal mucosa 9"},{"accession":"GSM153838","title":"Cancer 13"},{"accession":"GSM153821","title":"Normal mucosa 5"},{"accession":"GSM153841","title":"Normal mucosa 15"},{"accession":"GSM153832","title":"Cancer 10"},{"accession":"GSM153844","title":"Cancer 16"},{"accession":"GSM153835","title":"Normal mucosa 12"},{"accession":"GSM153818","title":"Cancer 3"},{"accession":"GSM153847","title":"Normal mucosa 18"},{"accession":"GSM153830","title":"Cancer 9"},{"accession":"GSM153850","title":"Cancer 19"},{"accession":"GSM153824","title":"Cancer 6"},{"accession":"GSM153827","title":"Normal mucosa 8"},{"accession":"GSM153856","title":"Cancer 22"},{"accession":"GSM153839","title":"Normal mucosa 14"},{"accession":"GSM153833","title":"Normal mucosa 11"},{"accession":"GSM153853","title":"Normal mucosa 21"},{"accession":"GSM153836","title":"Cancer 12"},{"accession":"GSM153822","title":"Cancer 5"},{"accession":"GSM153825","title":"Normal mucosa 7"},{"accession":"GSM153813","title":"Normal mucosa 1"},{"accession":"GSM153816","title":"Cancer 2"},{"accession":"GSM153819","title":"Normal mucosa 4"},{"accession":"GSM153842","title":"Cancer 15"},{"accession":"GSM153845","title":"Normal mucosa 17"}],"relations":[],"extrelations":[],"n_samples":44,"seriestitle":"","platformtitle":"","platformtaxa":"","samplestaxa":"","pubmedids":["15170515"],"projects":[],"ftplink":"ftp://ftp.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/geo/series/GSE6nnn/GSE6631/","geo2r":"yes","bioproject":"PRJNA98861"},"200002837":{"uid":"200002837","accession":"GSE2837","gds":"","title":"Survival Prediction of HNSCC using FFPE samples","summary":"Recently it was shown that gene expression signatures generated from DNA microarray analyses have promise as biomarkers of clinical outcome and that the molecular characteristics of tumors could be elucidated. Through this study, we have determined a high-risk signature for recurrence as a prognostic biomarker using formalin-fixed HNSCC tumors and tested the results to an independent data set obtained from fresh frozen tumors as a comparison. Also, we have shown the genes that are involved in epithelial to mesenchymal transition and nuclear factor-ĸB signaling deregulation are the most prominent molecular characteristics of the high-risk tumors. Forty samples including 34 formalin-fixed tissues and 6 matched frozen tissues from 29 HNSCC patients were analyzed for gene expression. The formalin-fixed tumors were classified based on their gene expression by intrinsic analysis and the intrinsic gene list was tested on the classification of previously published 60 frozen HNSCC tumors which highly correlated. Based on the molecular classification, a 75-gene list that is predictive of high-risk for recurrence was determined by training on the formalin-fixed tumor set and tested on the independent frozen tumor set. The difference in recurrence-free survival (RFS) between the high-risk vs. low-risk groups in the training and test sets were statistically significant (Log-rank test, p=0.002 and p=0.03, respectively). Also, the gene expression data was interrogated using Gene Set Enrichment Analysis to determine functional significance. The most significant sets of genes that are enriched in the high-risk tumors were genes involving epithelial to mesenchymal transition (EMT), NF-ĸB activation and cell adhesion. In conclusion, global gene expression analysis is feasible using formalin-fixed tissue, and the data from different sample preparations and array platforms can be reliably combined for analyses. The 75-gene list can be utilized as a prognostic biomarker of recurrence and the molecular characteristics of EMT and NF-ĸB activation can be targeted as the novel therapy in the identified high-risk patients.  Keywords: FFPE, survival analysis","gpl":"1352","gse":"2837","taxon":"Homo sapiens","entrytype":"GSE","gdstype":"Expression profiling by array","ptechtype":"","valtype":"","ssinfo":"","subsetinfo":"","pdat":"2006/11/02","suppfile":"","samples":[{"accession":"GSM62201","title":"2004-06-08-300148-Chung-HumanX3P-Rep1"},{"accession":"GSM62221","title":"2005-01-14-CHC96-Chung-HumanX3P-Rep1"},{"accession":"GSM62204","title":"2004-11-24-CHC76-Chung-HumanX3P-Rep1"},{"accession":"GSM62224","title":"2005-03-16-CHC148-Chung-HumanX3P-Rep1"},{"accession":"GSM101385","title":"2005-10-20-81CC45-Chung-HumanX3P-Rep1"},{"accession":"GSM62227","title":"2005-06-02-81CC2-Chung-HuX3P-Rep1"},{"accession":"GSM62210","title":"2004-12-16-CHC83-Chung-HumanX3P-Rep1"},{"accession":"GSM62213","title":"2004-12-16-CHC86-Chung-HumanX3P-Rep1"},{"accession":"GSM101377","title":"2005-07-19-81CC30-Chung-HuX3P-Rep1"},{"accession":"GSM62207","title":"2004-11-24-CHC80-Chung-HumanX3P-Rep1"},{"accession":"GSM101383","title":"2005-10-20-81CC44-Chung-HumanX3P-Rep1"},{"accession":"GSM101380","title":"2005-10-20-81CC41-Chung-HumanX3P-Rep1"},{"accession":"GSM62219","title":"2005-01-12-CHC94-Chung-HumanX3P-Rep1"},{"accession":"GSM62228","title":"2005-06-02-81CC3-Chung-HuX3P-Rep1"},{"accession":"GSM101372","title":"2005-07-19-81CC25-Chung-HuX3P-Rep1"},{"accession":"GSM62202","title":"2004-06-08-S0324497-Chung-HumanX3P-Rep1"},{"accession":"GSM62216","title":"2005-01-12-CHC91-Chung-HumanX3P-Rep1"},{"accession":"GSM62205","title":"2004-11-24-CHC77-Chung-HumanX3P-Rep1"},{"accession":"GSM62208","title":"2004-11-24-CHC81-Chung-HumanX3P-Rep1"},{"accession":"GSM101375","title":"2005-07-19-81CC26-Chung-HuX3P-Rep1"},{"accession":"GSM62211","title":"2004-12-16-CHC84-Chung-HumanX3P-Rep1"},{"accession":"GSM101381","title":"2005-10-20-81CC43-Chung-HumanX3P